---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { getExperience, includedExperiences, experienceFrame } from '../../../data/experiences';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths';
const caseIII = getExperience('case-iii');
export function getStaticPaths() { return includedExperiences.some(e => e.slug === 'case-iii') ? [{ params: { trainer: 'case-iii' } }] : []; }
const trainerHref = sitePath(experienceFrame(caseIII));
const contents = [
  ['overview', 'What Case III is'],
  ['at-a-glance', 'The recovery at a glance'],
  ['clearance', 'The Marshal clearance'],
  ['tacan', 'Point-to-point TACAN navigation'],
  ['entry', 'Entering the marshal hold'],
  ['eat', 'Holding and making EAT'],
  ['penetration', 'Commencing, platform and the descent'],
  ['final', 'Configuration, final and the ball'],
  ['check', 'Check your understanding'],
  ['launch', 'Launch the trainer'],
  ['limits', 'Scope and limits'],
  ['sources', 'Sources and unresolved review'],
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Case III recovery" activeSection="manual" activeSubject="naval-operations"
  compactSubjectNav={true}
  breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Naval Operations',href:sitePath('manual/naval-operations/')},{label:'Case III'}]}>
  <section class="status-panel" data-experience-id={caseIII.id} data-experience-revision={caseIII.revision}>
    <h2>{caseIII.maturity}</h2>
    <p>Revision {caseIII.revision} · {caseIII.applicability} No source-backed aviation review has validated this module or its trainer.</p>
    <p>This module walks through the whole recovery the {caseIII.title} trains: {caseIII.objective.charAt(0).toLowerCase() + caseIII.objective.slice(1)}</p>
    <p>Prerequisites: {caseIII.prerequisites.join(' · ')}.</p>
  </section>

  <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title">
    <h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
    <ol>{contents.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="overview">What Case III is</h2>
  <p>In outline, Case III is the carrier recovery used when darkness or weather rules out a visual approach to the ship. Instead of joining overhead, each aircraft is cleared to a holding fix, leaves it at an assigned time and flies a controlled descent onto an instrument final approach, so spacing comes from timing rather than from seeing other aircraft. This outline is unverified: the 2009 CV NATOPS manual named in the <a href="#sources">sources</a> is a research lead whose passages have not been checked for this revision. The details below describe how the trainer models the recovery.</p>
  <p>Every number on this page is the trainer’s exercise setpoint unless a source is named beside it. Speeds are KIAS (knots indicated airspeed), distances are nautical miles (NM), DME (distance measuring equipment) values are TACAN (tactical air navigation) ranges from the carrier, and headings, radials and courses are magnetic. The trainer has no wind, so in this module a heading equals the track over the ground.</p>

  <h2 id="at-a-glance">The recovery at a glance</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="glance-caption" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <caption id="glance-caption">Case III phases as modelled by the trainer. Values are exercise setpoints, not verified procedure or aircraft limits.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Phase</th><th scope="col">What you do</th><th scope="col">Trainer setpoint</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">1. Check in</th><td>Call Marshal inbound and read back the clearance.</td><td>Marshal radial, 21 DME, 6,000 ft, EAT</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">2. Navigate</th><td>Fly point to point from your radial/DME to the marshal fix.</td><td>6,000 ft, 250 KIAS</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">3. Enter the hold</th><td>Join the racetrack at the fix; report established.</td><td>Left-hand, 6:00 circuit</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">4. Hold</th><td>Adjust the pattern so you leave the fix inbound at EAT.</td><td>Within 10 s of EAT</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">5. Commence</th><td>Call Commencing, descend toward the ship on BRC, switch to Approach.</td><td>250 KIAS, 4,000 ft/min</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">6. Platform</th><td>Report Platform and reduce the descent rate.</td><td>5,000 ft, then 2,000 ft/min</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">7. Level and intercept</th><td>Level off and intercept the final bearing.</td><td>1,200 ft, final = BRC − 9°</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">8. Configure</th><td>Slow down, then gear, flaps and hook; on-speed.</td><td>200 KIAS at 10 DME; configure at 8 DME; on-speed by 6 DME</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">9. Glidepath</th><td>Intercept and follow the glidepath.</td><td>About 3 NM</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">10. Ball and landing</th><td>Call the ball, respond to the LSO, trap or wave off.</td><td>Ball call about 0.75 NM</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>In the trainer, BRC (base recovery course) is the carrier’s heading during the recovery, and the angled landing area is modelled 9° left of it, so the final bearing is BRC − 9°. Neither the definition nor the offset for a particular carrier has been verified against a source.</p>

  <h2 id="clearance">The Marshal clearance</h2>
  <p>In the trainer, Marshal’s clearance gives you everything you need before you descend: a radial, a DME, an altitude (“angels 6” is 6,000 ft in the trainer’s dialogue) and an expected approach time (EAT). The trainer’s Marshal says, for example, “Marshal mother’s 220 radial, 21 DME, angels 6. Expected approach time 16:21.” That wording is inherited exercise dialogue, not verified Navy phraseology.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A radial is a magnetic bearing from the station.</strong> The FAA Pilot/Controller Glossary defines it as a magnetic bearing extending from a VOR, VORTAC or TACAN facility. Here the station is Mother, the carrier’s TACAN.</li>
    <li><strong>Radial plus DME is a position.</strong> The marshal fix is where the assigned radial crosses the assigned DME ring.</li>
    <li><strong>The inbound course is the reciprocal.</strong> To fly from the fix toward Mother you fly the radial’s reciprocal: add or subtract 180° and wrap into 000–359°. The 220 radial gives an inbound course of 040°.</li>
    <li><strong>The trainer puts the fix directly astern.</strong> Its marshal radial is always BRC + 180°, so the inbound course equals BRC. In DCS, use the actual clearance you receive.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tacan">Point-to-point TACAN navigation</h2>
  <p>You rarely start on the marshal radial. TACAN tells you where you are (your radial and DME) but not the direction to a fix that is somewhere else. Point-to-point navigation is the skill of turning two radial/DME positions into a heading.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Find your own radial.</strong> On the HSI the bearing pointer’s head points to Mother and its tail sits on your radial. Your radial is not your heading.</li>
    <li><strong>Picture the plot.</strong> Imagine a compass rose centred on Mother. Plot yourself on your radial at your DME and the fix on its radial at its DME. The larger DME sets the edge of the picture.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the direction of the line.</strong> Draw a line from you to the fix. Slide that line, without turning it, until it passes through the centre of the rose: where it points on the rose is the heading to fly.</li>
    <li><strong>Fly it and refine.</strong> Every minute or so, re-plot from your new radial and DME and correct the heading. Judge the combined position, not each number alone: DME is your distance from Mother, not from the fix. On the worked example below, the direct track passes inside 21 DME before the range opens back out to the fix, and that is correct.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Avoid two common slips: flying the fix radial (that is a direction from Mother, not from you), and flying the inbound course before you have reached the radial. DME is slant range, not horizontal distance (FAA AIM 1-1-7). At 6,000 ft, about 1 NM above the ship, the difference at 21 DME is under 0.03 NM, so the module treats DME as horizontal range.</p>

  <section class="lesson" data-lesson="navigation" aria-labelledby="nav-title">
    <h3 id="nav-title">Practise: heading to the marshal fix</h3>
    <p>Set the ship’s BRC and your own position, estimate the heading to the fix, then check it. The fix is on the BRC + 180° radial as in the trainer.</p>
    <div class="lesson-grid">
      <div class="controls">
        <label>BRC (°)<input name="brc" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="0" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <p class="derived">Marshal radial <strong data-fix-radial>180</strong>°</p>
        <label>Fix DME (NM)<input name="fixDme" type="number" min="1" max="60" step="1" value="21" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your radial (°)<input name="youRadial" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="90" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your DME (NM)<input name="youDme" type="number" min="0" max="80" step="1" value="30" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <label>Your heading estimate (°)<input name="guess" type="number" min="0" max="359" step="1" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <div class="buttons">
          <button type="button" data-check>Check estimate</button>
          <button type="button" data-reveal>Reveal</button>
          <button type="button" data-new>New problem</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 320" role="img" aria-label="Compass plot centred on Mother"></svg>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p data-result aria-live="polite">Worked example: from the 090 radial at 30 DME to the 180 radial at 21 DME, the direct track is about 235° for 36.6 NM.</p>
    <noscript><p>The interactive plot needs JavaScript. The worked example above shows the same calculation.</p></noscript>
  </section>
  <details>
    <summary>How the check is calculated</summary>
    <p>Place Mother at the origin with x east and y north. A position on radial <var>r</var> at <var>d</var> NM is x = <var>d</var>·sin <var>r</var>, y = <var>d</var>·cos <var>r</var>. The track from you to the fix is the direction of the difference vector, atan2(Δx, Δy), and the distance is its length. In the worked example you are at (30, 0) and the fix at (0, −21), so Δx = −30, Δy = −21: the track is 180° + atan(30/21) ≈ 235.0° and the distance √1341 ≈ 36.6 NM. With no wind, that track is also the heading. The time shown uses the trainer’s fixed map rate of 250 NM/hour, which is not a groundspeed model.</p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="entry">Entering the marshal hold</h2>
  <p>The marshal hold is a racetrack anchored on the fix. Its inbound leg runs along the inbound course to the fix; you turn at the fix onto the outbound leg and turn again back to the inbound leg. The trainer flies a left-hand pattern: two 180° turns of 2:00 each and two 1:00 straight legs, a 6:00 circuit. These are the trainer’s fixed hold parameters.</p>
  <p>How you join the racetrack depends on your heading as you reach the fix. The FAA AIM (5-3-8 j.3) describes three entries for instrument holding:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Direct:</strong> fly to the fix and turn to follow the pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Teardrop:</strong> at the fix turn outbound to a heading 30° toward the holding side of the outbound course, fly it for one minute, then turn in the direction of the hold to intercept the inbound course.</li>
    <li><strong>Parallel:</strong> at the fix turn to parallel the outbound course on the non-holding side for one minute, then turn back toward the holding side through more than 180° to return to the fix or intercept the inbound course.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The entry is chosen from your heading on arrival at the fix, and the AIM treats ±5° of heading as acceptable when choosing (5-3-8 j.8(d)). The AIM shows the sectors in a figure that this revision has not checked. The module uses the conventional reading of it: measured from the inbound course in the direction of the hold’s turns, an arrival heading 110° to 180° round is a teardrop, 180° to 290° is a parallel, and everything else (a 180° sector) is direct. A left-hand hold mirrors a right-hand one.</p>
  <p class="note"><strong>Applicability:</strong> these are FAA civil instrument holding entries applied to the trainer’s marshal hold as a general instrument-flying skill. Whether CV NATOPS prescribes these entries for a Case III marshal pattern has not been verified. The trainer itself joins the hold automatically when you select <em>Enter marshal hold</em>; it does not fly these manoeuvres.</p>

  <section class="lesson" data-lesson="entry" aria-labelledby="entry-title">
    <h3 id="entry-title">Practise: choose the entry</h3>
    <p>Set the inbound course and your heading at the fix, choose an entry, then check it. The plot shows the racetrack, the sector you arrive from and your arrival track.</p>
    <div class="lesson-grid">
      <div class="controls">
        <fieldset class="inline">
          <legend>Hold direction</legend>
          <label><input type="radio" name="turns" value="left" checked /> Left (trainer)</label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="turns" value="right" /> Right</label>
        </fieldset>
        <label>Inbound course <output data-inbound-value>000°</output><input name="inbound" type="range" min="0" max="350" step="10" value="0" /></label>
        <label>Heading at the fix <output data-heading-value>090°</output><input name="heading" type="range" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="90" /></label>
        <fieldset class="inline">
          <legend>Your entry</legend>
          <label><input type="radio" name="answer" value="direct" /> Direct</label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="answer" value="teardrop" /> Teardrop</label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="answer" value="parallel" /> Parallel</label>
        </fieldset>
        <div class="buttons">
          <button type="button" data-check>Check entry</button>
          <button type="button" data-new>New problem</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 320" role="img" aria-label="Holding entry sectors around the marshal fix"></svg>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p data-result aria-live="polite">Worked example: left-hand hold, inbound course 000°, arriving on heading 090°. Measured anticlockwise from 000°, 090° is 270° round, so this is a parallel entry: turn to 180° on the non-holding side, then turn right back toward the hold.</p>
    <noscript><p>The interactive plot needs JavaScript. The worked example above applies the same rule.</p></noscript>
  </section>

  <h2 id="eat">Holding and making EAT</h2>
  <p>In the trainer, EAT is the time you leave the fix inbound and begin the descent. In outline (unverified, see the CV NATOPS lead below), each aircraft’s EAT differs and hitting it is what keeps the approach spaced. For FAA civil holding, the AIM likewise expects pilots told when to leave a fix to adjust the pattern to leave at that time (5-3-8 k). In the trainer you must call Commencing at the fix within 10 seconds of EAT, and EATs are whole minutes on a clock that starts at 16:00Z.</p>
  <p>Hold time is adjustable, so plan it as soon as you know when you will reach the fix:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Work out the time available</strong> from reaching the fix to EAT.</li>
    <li><strong>Count whole circuits.</strong> Each standard trainer circuit is 6:00.</li>
    <li><strong>Absorb the remainder in one circuit.</strong> The two turns take a fixed 4:00, so change only the straight legs. With no wind the outbound and inbound legs take equal time, and every extra second on the outbound leg adds about two seconds to the circuit. Extend the last circuit, or fly an extra short one if at least 4:00 remains.</li>
    <li><strong>Check on every pass of the fix</strong> and correct the next outbound leg if you are early or late.</li>
    <li><strong>If you cannot make it,</strong> the trainer expects you to ask Marshal for an amended EAT rather than leave late.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="note"><strong>Model limits:</strong> this planner uses the trainer’s no-wind geometry. The trainer itself only flies standard circuits and lets the aircraft wait at the fix, which a real aircraft cannot do; it does not let you change leg lengths, speed or turn rate. Real holding corrections for wind and the CV NATOPS EAT guidance (a §6.4.4 lead) have not been verified.</p>

  <section class="lesson" data-lesson="eat" aria-labelledby="eat-title">
    <h3 id="eat-title">Practise: plan the hold</h3>
    <p>Enter when you will reach the fix and your EAT, using HH:MM or HH:MM:SS.</p>
    <div class="controls row">
      <label>At the fix (UTC)<input name="arrive" type="text" value="16:08:20" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" /></label>
      <label>EAT (UTC)<input name="eat" type="text" value="16:21" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" /></label>
    </div>
    <svg class="timeline" viewBox="0 0 320 64" role="img" aria-label="Hold timeline"></svg>
    <div data-result aria-live="polite"><p>Worked example: reaching the fix at 16:08:20 for a 16:21 EAT leaves 12:40. Two standard circuits take 12:00, so 0:40 is left over. That is too short for an extra circuit, so fly one standard circuit and then one stretched circuit of 6:40, with 1:20 outbound and inbound legs instead of 1:00.</p></div>
    <noscript><p>The planner needs JavaScript. The worked example above shows the method.</p></noscript>
  </section>

  <h2 id="penetration">Commencing, platform and the descent</h2>
  <p>At EAT, leaving the fix inbound, call Commencing on Marshal. In the trainer you then turn to BRC, hold 250 KIAS and descend toward 1,200 ft at 4,000 ft/min. Marshal hands you to Approach, and you check in before platform.</p>
  <p><strong>In the trainer, platform is an altitude, not a distance.</strong> At 5,000 ft report Platform and reduce the descent to 2,000 ft/min, continuing to 1,200 ft. Level there, and intercept the final bearing (BRC − 9° in the trainer).</p>
  <figure>
    <svg class="profile" viewBox="0 0 640 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="profile-caption">
      <g class="grid">
        <line x1="60" y1="190" x2="620" y2="190" />
        <line x1="60" y1="20" x2="60" y2="190" />
        {[0, 3, 6, 10, 12, 15, 18, 21].map(d => <g><line x1={620 - d * (560 / 21)} y1="190" x2={620 - d * (560 / 21)} y2="196" /><text x={620 - d * (560 / 21)} y="210" text-anchor="middle">{d}</text></g>)}
        {[1200, 5000, 6000].map(a => <g><line x1="54" y1={190 - a * (160 / 6000)} x2="60" y2={190 - a * (160 / 6000)} /><text x="50" y={194 - a * (160 / 6000)} text-anchor="end">{a.toLocaleString('en-US')}</text></g>)}
        <text x="340" y="226" text-anchor="middle">DME from Mother (NM)</text>
        <text x="14" y="105" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 14 105)">Altitude (ft)</text>
      </g>
      <polyline class="path" points={[[21, 6000], [19.96, 5000], [12.04, 1200], [3, 1200], [0, 0]].map(([d, a]) => `${620 - d * (560 / 21)},${190 - a * (160 / 6000)}`).join(' ')} />
      <g class="marks">
        <text x="70" y="22">Marshal fix · 21 DME · 6,000 ft</text>
        <text x="90" y="50">Platform 5,000 ft ≈ 20 DME</text>
        <text x="300" y="150">Level 1,200 ft ≈ 12 DME</text>
        <line x1={620 - 10 * (560 / 21)} y1="158" x2={620 - 10 * (560 / 21)} y2="166" />
        <line x1={620 - 8 * (560 / 21)} y1="158" x2={620 - 8 * (560 / 21)} y2="166" />
        <text x="440" y="120">10 DME slow</text>
        <text x="440" y="136">8 DME configure</text>
        <text x="540" y="182">Glidepath ≈ 3 NM</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption id="profile-caption">Trainer vertical profile with no wind, drawn to scale from the setpoints above. Leaving 21 DME at 6,000 ft and 250 NM/hour, the 1,000 ft to platform at 4,000 ft/min takes 15 s, about 1.0 NM, so platform is reached near 20 DME. The 3,800 ft from platform to 1,200 ft at 2,000 ft/min takes 1:54, about 7.9 NM, so the aircraft levels near 12 DME. These are exercise numbers, not a published profile.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="final">Configuration, final and the ball</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>10 DME:</strong> begin slowing to 200 KIAS, level at 1,200 ft on the final bearing.</li>
    <li><strong>8 DME:</strong> gear, flaps and hook down; select on-speed and DLC (direct lift control). Be configured and on-speed by 6 DME. The trainer’s 135 KIAS on-speed value is an exercise setpoint, not a verified F-14 approach speed or angle-of-attack value.</li>
    <li><strong>About 3 NM:</strong> intercept the glidepath and follow the needles. In the trainer, Approach says “fly bullseye” once you are aligned.</li>
    <li><strong>About 0.75 NM:</strong> call the ball on the LSO (landing signal officer) frequency. The trainer’s LSO then calls your line-up and glidepath deviations.</li>
    <li><strong>Touchdown:</strong> the trainer simulates a three-wire arrestment when you are configured, on speed and on centreline; otherwise it waves you off and you climb on the final bearing. Bolters, wind over deck and ACLS (automatic carrier landing system) exchanges are not simulated.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
  <form class="lesson quiz" data-lesson="quiz" aria-labelledby="quiz-title" onsubmit="return false">
    <h3 id="quiz-title">Five questions</h3>
    <fieldset data-answer="b">
      <legend>1. Marshal clears you to the 220 radial, 21 DME. What is your inbound course from the fix?</legend>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="a" /> 220°</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="b" /> 040°</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="c" /> 310°</label>
      <p data-explanation hidden><strong></strong>The inbound course is the radial’s reciprocal: 220° − 180° = 040°.</p>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset data-answer="b">
      <legend>2. You are on the 090 radial at 30 DME. The fix is the 180 radial at 21 DME. Which heading is closest to the direct track?</legend>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="a" /> 180°, the fix radial</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="b" /> 235°</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="c" /> 270°, toward Mother</label>
      <p data-explanation hidden><strong></strong>The fix lies south-west of you. The line from (30, 0) to (0, −21) points about 235°. The fix radial is a direction from Mother, not from you.</p>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset data-answer="c">
      <legend>3. In a left-hand hold with an inbound course of 040°, you reach the fix on heading 130°. Which entry fits?</legend>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="a" /> Direct</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="b" /> Teardrop</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="c" /> Parallel</label>
      <p data-explanation hidden><strong></strong>Measured anticlockwise (the left-hand hold’s turn direction) from 040°, heading 130° is 270° round, inside the 180°–290° parallel sector.</p>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset data-answer="a">
      <legend>4. You reach the fix with 9:30 to EAT. Trainer circuits are 6:00, with 4:00 of turns. What works?</legend>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="a" /> One stretched circuit with 2:45 legs</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="b" /> One standard circuit, then leave 3:30 early</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="c" /> Two standard circuits</label>
      <p data-explanation hidden><strong></strong>9:30 is one circuit plus 3:30, too short for a second circuit. Stretching one circuit to 9:30 leaves 5:30 for the two legs, 2:45 each.</p>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset data-answer="b">
      <legend>5. What changes at platform in the trainer?</legend>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="a" /> You slow to 200 KIAS</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="b" /> You report Platform and reduce the descent from 4,000 to 2,000 ft/min</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="c" /> You lower the gear</label>
      <p data-explanation hidden><strong></strong>Platform is 5,000 ft. Speed and configuration changes come later, at 10 and 8 DME.</p>
    </fieldset>
    <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-check>Check answers</button></div>
    <p data-score role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </form>

  <h2 id="launch">Launch the trainer</h2>
  <p>The trainer puts all of this together in one sortie: Marshal check-in, point-to-point navigation, the hold and EAT, the penetration, final and the ball. Direct the pilot using heading, airspeed, altitude and vertical-rate commands. Read the sortie popup, then select Start Exercise. Instructor mode gives cues and pauses at decisions; switch it off to rehearse without hints. Voice is optional and uses your browser’s speech voices.</p>
  <p>The trainer needs a whole window, so it always opens on its own rather than inside this page.</p>
  <p class="launch">
    <a class="launch-button" href={trainerHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-experience-launch data-experience-id={caseIII.id}>Launch the Case III trainer<span> (opens in a new window)</span></a>
  </p>
  <p>{caseIII.maturity}. Revision {caseIII.revision}.</p>
  <noscript><p>The trainer requires JavaScript. This module’s text, worked examples and sources remain readable without it.</p></noscript>

  <h2 id="limits">Scope and limits</h2>
  <p>Aircraft dynamics, AoA, fuel consumption, glidepath and arrestment are synthetic. Outside the hold, the airspeed number is also used as NM/hour for map movement; IAS-to-TAS conversion and wind are not modeled. Hold geometry uses a fixed synthetic 250 NM/hour. Every airspeed, altitude, vertical rate, DME fix, timing and configuration gate shown in the trainer and this module (for example 250 KIAS at the 6,000 ft, 21 DME marshal, the 4,000 then 2,000 ft/min descent, and the 10 and 8 DME slow-down and configuration points) is an exercise setpoint. None has a verified source locator, and none is a verified procedure or aircraft limitation. The night-themed scenario does not model date, lighting or visibility conditions. This is not an approved operating procedure or a proficiency assessment. The exact DCS version has not been verified. Radio dialogue and scoring criteria are inherited exercise content, not verified Navy phraseology or measures of proficiency.</p>
  <p>The module’s calculations (point-to-point track, entry sector and hold plan) are simplified educational models: flat-earth plotting around Mother, DME as horizontal range, no wind, and the trainer’s fixed turns and legs. The 2009 CV NATOPS citation in the source register is a historical research lead only: its cited passages, current applicability and claim mapping have not been verified. Fixed turns and legs are synthetic exercise parameters, not a claim about a prescribed hold.</p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources and unresolved review</h2>
  <p>The references below are not a claim-to-source map. No applicable Navy or DCS passage has been verified for this module’s or the trainer’s Case III procedure, numeric gates, radio dialogue or scoring. A revision-3 review inspected limited Heatblur F-14 and CASE I passages for DCS-specific context only; those do not validate this exercise. References do not certify procedures or grant rights to reproduce manuals. No manual files or third-party media are included.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://info.publicintelligence.net/CV-NATOPS-JUL09.pdf">NAVAIR 00-80T-105, CV NATOPS, 31 July 2009</a>, §§6.4.1.1 and 6.4.4, pp. 6-6 and 6-8: historical leads for the Case III overview, hold and EAT claims only; the passages and current applicability were not verified.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap5_section_3.html">FAA Aeronautical Information Manual</a>, ¶5-3-8 j.3 (holding entry procedures), j.8(d) (±5° heading tolerance when choosing an entry), k (adjusting the pattern to leave the fix at a specified time) and the entry-sector figure (cited in the text as FIG 5-3-4, captioned FIG 5-3-8), accessed 24 October 2026: supports the entry definitions and timing adjustment for FAA civil instrument holding. The 70°/110°/180° sector sizes are the conventional reading of the figure, which this revision did not inspect. It does not establish Case III marshal procedure.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap1_section_1.html">FAA Aeronautical Information Manual</a>, ¶1-1-7 (DME), accessed 24 October 2026: DME distance is slant range, not horizontal distance.</li>
